    "### Hypothesis 1 conclusion: Demographics → partly supported\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Age** has a strong effect on the seasonal vaccine (28.5% for 18-34 vs 67.4% for 65+), but only a small one on H1N1 (19.0% - 24.3%).\n",
    "- **Education:** college graduates are vaccinated more often than people with < 12 years of school (H1N1 24.6% vs 16.7%, seasonal 51.1% vs 40.1%).\n",
    "- **Race:** Black respondents have the lowest uptake (H1N1 14.9%, seasonal 35.0%) compared with White respondents (21.9%, 49.1%).\n",
    "- **Child under 6 months:** clearly higher H1N1 uptake (30.5% vs 20.5%), while seasonal is almost the same.\n",
    "- **Sex, marital status, household size:** smaller differences. Larger households have lower seasonal uptake, which is probably linked to younger age.\n",
    "- **Verdict:** demographics matter, mainly for the seasonal vaccine. For H1N1 the differences are small."
