   "source": [
    "### Hypothesis 4 conclusion: Health literacy and behaviour → supported (mostly through opinions)\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Opinions have the clearest effect.** People who rate the H1N1 vaccine as very effective have 40.5% H1N1 uptake, versus 4.7% for \"not at all effective\". For the seasonal vaccine the gap is 70.5% vs 15.8%.\n",
    "- **Perceived risk:** uptake rises steadily with perceived risk (H1N1 8.8% → 51.1%, seasonal 20.9% → 74.5%).\n",
    "- **Health workers** are about twice as likely to get the H1N1 vaccine (40.9% vs 18.9%).\n",
    "- **Concern and knowledge** both increase uptake (H1N1: concern 13.6% → 29.2%, knowledge 14.4% → 27.8%).\n",
    "- **Preventive behaviours** (hand washing, face mask, avoidance, etc.) show only small positive differences of a few percentage points.\n",
    "- **Fear of getting sick from the vaccine** shows no clear trend. The lowest uptake is at value 3 (\"Don't know\"), not at the extremes.\n",
    "- **Verdict:** beliefs about vaccine effectiveness and risk matter much more than everyday preventive behaviour."
   ]
